{{also|Politesse}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|fr|politesse}}, from {{der|en|it|politezza}}, from {{m|it|polito}}, past participle of {{m|it|pulire||to clean}}, from  {{der|en|la|polire|}}, present active infinite form of {{m|la|poliō||I polish}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/pɒlɪˈtɛs/}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/pɑləˈtɛs/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# [[civility|Civility]], [[politeness]], [[courtesy]] or [[gallantry]]; or an instance of this.
#* {{quote-song|en|year=1968|author=Keith Richards; Mick Jagger|title=Sympathy for the Devil|artist=Rolling Stones|album=Beggars Banquet|passage=So if you meet me, have some courtesy / Have some sympathy, and some taste / Use all your well-learned '''politesse''' / Or I'll lay your soul to waste}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1978|author=w:Iris Murdoch|title=The Sea, The Sea|publisher=Vintage|year_published=1999|pages=56–57|passage=The reference in his letter to ‘having a drink’ is of course just an empty '''politesse'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2011|author=Thomas Penn|title=Winter King|publisher=Penguin|year_published=2012|page=7|passage=The soft '''politesse''' concealed a sharp observer, a gleaner of information, cool under pressure and used to having to think several steps ahead {{...}}}}

